	NT=|Angloman is a political parody comic in book format, published by a mainstream literary
	press. It is a speculative work which uses cliches and tropes of superhero comic
	books to mock Quebec and Canadian politics, primarily by casting well-known politicians
	as superheroic characters to mock their pretensions and belief systems. Angloman
	consists of five loosely connected short comics stories --written by Shainblum and
	illustrated by Morrissette-- as well as a foreword by well-known Canadian political
	cartoonist "Aislin" (Terry Mosher), and four pages of text and art biographies of
	the various characters. Angloman was a minor hit upon its release in late 1995, immediately
	after the last Quebec independence referendum, and spent 22 weeks on the Montreal
	Gazette bestseller list. A sequel, Angloman 2, is in the works and is scheduled for
	release in November/December 1996.|
	SY=|Angloman, in reality Eaton M. McGill, insurance underwriter for SunLife Assurance,
	is your typical everyday superhero. Bigger than life, a champion of bilingualism
	and tolerance, and dumb as a post. Angloman's secret weapons include his indestructible
	Stop/Arret shield, his deadly apostrophe darts, and his supersonic Angloplane. His
	secret base of operations is the Fortress of Two Solitudes, hidden deep below the
	SunLife building.|
}
